dded a `close-issue` job, since nothing was ever closing the tracking issue when drift cleared.\n\n**The thing you were right to be wary of wouldn't have bought much anyway.** An agent-drafted PR needs an `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` in repo secrets — but that was never the binding constraint. The probes that actually *earn* a pin need both target CLIs installed, a TTY for the interactive smoke test, and **paid model credentials for each provider** (OpenAI for Codex, Anthropic for Claude) to make the recall turns real. An agent in CI would hit that same wall; it would just hit it more expensively and with more credentials sitting in your repo. So CI's honest ceiling is \"here's the one-line change and the checklist,\" and a human runs the probes and undrafts. I removed the old stage-2 TODO in favor of this.\n\nOne judgment call to flag: this does mean CI pushes branches (`contents: write`), which is the milder cousin of the concern you raised. It's the second commit in the PR, so it's droppable independently if you'd rather keep CI read-only and just take the revalidation.\n\nIssue #2 stays open until you merge — then the next daily run detects no drift and closes it automatically, which doubles as the new job proving itself. One leftover: a real (not fabricated) 9-line Codex session from my structural check sits in `~/.codex/sessions/2026/08/02/`; it's cwd-scoped to a scratch dir so it won't surface in your picker, and I left it rather than deleting from your session store.\n\nresult:\nRevalidated the Codex adapter against 0.146.0 (format unchanged, all four probes green), widened the pin, and made the drift workflow open a mechanical draft PR and auto-close its tracking issue — no API key required. Draft PR #4 opened, closes issue #2.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"worktree-codex-0146-revalidation","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge/.claude/worktrees/codex-0146-revalidation","head":"53a197349747a7a9d0e930bcbce72f6d25b0cd93","r