{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"Here's the landscape. I read the roadmap in `docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md`, the open GitHub issue, the sibling `conversation-ledger` repo state, and confirmed current CLI versions and test status (77/77 passing, build clean).\n\n## Ranked next steps\n\n**1. Revalidate Codex 0.146.0 — live drift, open right now**\nIssue #2 fired today: `@openai/codex` is at 0.146.0, your adapter is pinned `[\"0.144.\", \"0.145.\"]` (`src/targets/codex.ts:22`), and your local install is still 0.145.0. Until this is widened, every Codex fabrication on an updated machine warns and offers bootstrap — the degraded path becomes the default for anyone who ran `npm update`. Tooling for this already exists (`smoke:interactive`, `probe-codex-content.mjs`, `probe-codex-function-call.mjs`). Highest priority because it's the only item that is actively breaking the product's main flow.\n\n**2. Unblock the npm release chain — but the blocker is upstream, not here**\nThe order is: fix `conversation-ledger`'s redaction flaw → publish the ledger → swap `file:` for a semver range → publish turnbridge. Both names are still unclaimed on npm (verified 404). The reason I'd put the redaction flaw *inside* this item rather than treating it as someone else's problem: the ledger's own scratch doc says `cledger redact` reports success while the value stays recoverable via one `git reflog` command, status **unfixed**. Turnbridge's sharing model in the design doc leans on \"the ledger redacts at capture and sync time.\" Publishing a cross-machine conversation-sharing tool while its redaction claim is false is the one thing here that could actually hurt a user. Dogfooding has now run ~10 days past the \"few days\" deferral, so the only real gate left is that fix.\n\n**3. Decide the shim default**\nYou decided on 2026-07-21 that `turnbridge shim install` stays opt-in but \"may become default later if it proves stable over a few weeks.\" That window has elapsed. Cheap decision, meaningful UX difference, and it's better made before a public release than after — flipping a PATH-shim default on existing users is a much worse move than shipping with it.\n\n**4. Tighten the Claude Code pin**\n`VALIDATED_VERSION_PREFIX = \"2.\"` (`src/targets/claude-code.ts:19`) accepts all of 2.x, while Codex uses a per-minor list. Consequence: the drift detector will never flag Claude until 3.0, so a format change in 2.2 ships silently as a \"validated\" fabrication. Asymmetry worth resolving — probably by matching Codex's per-minor prefix list.\n\n**5. Add a CI test job** — and note it's coupled to #2\nThere's only `adapter-drift.yml`; nothing runs `npm test` on push or PR. It can't run today, because `npm install` in CI would need to resolve `file:../conversation-ledger`. So this lands naturally the moment the ledger is published, and is close to free at that point.\n\n**6. Multi-hop encrypted-reasoning carry-over**\nCurrently reasoning replay is gated per-event on `producer.source === \"codex\"`, which means re-bridging a conversation (Codex → Claude → Codex) drops the original hop's reasoning blobs even though the lineage chain records exactly where they came from. Explicitly noted as deliberately-not-built. Real fidelity win, and it's the \"deepen the two CLIs\" work you said should come before a third target.\n\n**7. Adapter-drift automation stage 2**\nThe commented-out job in `.github/workflows/adapter-drift.yml` would hand drift issues to a Claude Code action that revalidates and drafts the PR. Needs an `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` secret and comfort with CI pushing branches. Its value scales with how often #1 recurs — and #1 just recurred, which is the argument for it.\n\n**8. A third CLI target**\nYou said you expect both deepening *and* broadening before release, and this is the broadening half. Deliberately behind #6 by your own 2026-07-21 decision.\n\n**9. The three unmeasured spec items** — histories past ~300 turns,